---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Sprite from '../components/Sprite.astro';
import { bookInfo } from '../data/timeline';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base page="about" title="About — The Golden Path">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="kicker">sources and method</p>
      <h1>About this chronicle</h1>
    </header>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Canon</h2>
      <p>
        The record is Frank Herbert's six Dune novels, with the appendices and glossary printed
        with Dune. Where Herbert left something vague, the main timeline leaves it vague too.
        Everything else is ranked below that, and drawn so you can always tell which is which:
      </p>
      <ol class="tier-list">
        <li class="pxbox"><b>1. Frank Herbert's novels.</b> Solid cards on the timeline.</li>
        <li class="pxbox"><b>2. Denis Villeneuve's films.</b> Coloured film notes on those cards, tagged by how they differ.</li>
        <li class="pxbox dashed"><b>3. The later books by Brian Herbert and Kevin J. Anderson.</b> Dashed cards and notes marked LATER BOOKS. A
          <span class="badge eu-badge eu-k-retcon">RETCON</span> tag means a later book changes or re-explains
          something Frank Herbert wrote, and the card says what he wrote. <span class="badge eu-badge eu-k-expands">EXPANDS</span>
          means it fills a gap without contradicting him.</li>
        <li class="pxbox dashed"><b>4. Other screen versions.</b> Lynch's 1984 film, the two Sci Fi Channel miniseries and
          HBO's Dune: Prophecy. Hidden on the timeline until you switch them on, because they often depart a long way from the books.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Character, faction, family and web pages use tiers 1 and 2 only. The full list of later
        books, every retcon and every screen version is on <a href={`${base}/beyond/`}>Beyond Herbert</a>.
      </p>
      <ul class="books-grid">
        {Object.entries(bookInfo).map(([k, b]) => (
          <li class="pxbox"><Sprite name="book" size={2} /><div><b>{b.title}</b><span>{b.year} · {b.note}</span></div></li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <h2>Films</h2>
      <p>
        Denis Villeneuve's Dune (2021) and Dune: Part Two (2024). Each film note is tagged
        <span class="badge k-changed">CHANGED</span>, <span class="badge k-cut">CUT</span>,
        <span class="badge k-added">ADDED</span> or <span class="badge k-kept">KEPT</span>.
      </p>

      <h2>Dates</h2>
      <p>
        The Imperial calendar counts years After Guild (A.G.), from the start of the Spacing
        Guild's monopoly. Herbert gives hard dates for the deep past (the Butlerian Jihad, the
        Battle of Corrin) and for the opening of Dune in 10,191 A.G. After that, each novel dates
        itself only relative to the one before: Messiah is twelve years after Dune, Children nine
        after that, God Emperor about 3,500 years later, Heretics about 1,500 after Leto's death.
        Those dates are shown as intervals, marked "≈", and drawn as hollow markers on the timeline.
        The scale ruler uses approximate absolute positions built from those intervals.
      </p>
      <p>
        The appendices to Dune give some absolute dates (for example Paul's birth and death) that
        do not line up exactly with counting the intervals between books. Where they disagree,
        this site follows the novels' own intervals, because those are what the stories state.
      </p>
      <h2>People, powers and the web</h2>
      <p>
        Every event is tagged with the characters and factions in it. The character and faction
        pages, and the intersection views on <a href={`${base}/web/`}>the web</a>, are all built from those
        tags, so a correction to an event updates every view that uses it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="credits">Credits and copyright</h2>
      <p>
        The Golden Path is a non-commercial fan site, made for reference and commentary. It is not
        affiliated with, sponsored by or endorsed by anyone named below. Every name, character, place
        and story described here belongs to its owners.
      </p>
      <ul class="credits">
        <li><b>Frank Herbert's novels.</b> Dune (1965), Dune Messiah, Children of Dune, God Emperor of Dune,
          Heretics of Dune and Chapterhouse: Dune, by Frank Herbert. The Dune property is owned by
          Herbert Properties LLC.</li>
        <li><b>The later novels.</b> By Brian Herbert and Kevin J. Anderson, published in the United States by
          Spectra and later Tor Books. Copyright their authors.</li>
        <li><b>Denis Villeneuve's films.</b> Dune (2021) and Dune: Part Two (2024), produced by Legendary
          Pictures and distributed by Warner Bros. Pictures.</li>
        <li><b>David Lynch's film.</b> Dune (1984), produced by the Dino De Laurentiis Corporation and
          distributed by Universal Pictures.</li>
        <li><b>The miniseries.</b> Frank Herbert's Dune (2000), produced by New Amsterdam Entertainment, Blixa
          Film Produktion and Victor Television Productions for the Sci Fi Channel, and Frank Herbert's
          Children of Dune (2003), made for the Sci Fi Channel.</li>
        <li><b>Dune: Prophecy.</b> The HBO series (2024), produced by Legendary Television with Herbert
          Properties and others.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The summaries on this site are written in our own words. Quotations from the novels are a
        sentence or two at most, and always say where they come from. No text, stills, posters or
        other images from the books, films or series are reproduced. Every picture here is original
        pixel art drawn by code when the site is built, and none of the portraits is based on an
        actor's likeness.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>This site's own work.</b> The source code is released under the MIT License. The original
        writing and pixel art are released under
        <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/" rel="license">Creative Commons
        Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0</a>, as far as they are ours to license. That licence does not
        cover the Dune names, characters and settings, which belong to their owners.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
